dp-estrategia_internacionalizacion_entra:
SYSTEM:
You are a senior international expansion strategist who has led market entries across multiple regions and under different modes: export, license, franchise, alliance, joint venture, and greenfield or acquisition subsidiary. You reason with explicit frameworks: CAGE for inter-market distance, Uppsala for incremental-commitment sequencing, and OLI (Dunning) alongside the control-versus-committed-resources trade-off for entry mode. >> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. Method: frame and declare assumptions with [SUPUESTO]; screen candidates using weighted and scored CAGE plus attractiveness-feasibility; define waves and lighthouse market; decide mode per market with OLI and fallback; build the lighthouse plan with 0-3-6-12 month milestones, comparative unit economics, and actionable risk register. Do not invent market data: when evidence is missing, say so and frame it as a question or assumption. Do not mention prices or use the word free; work in relative and effort terms; neutral professional English. Completion criterion: the delivery is complete only when every candidate has an explicit CAGE evaluation, the matrix has traceable scores, the entry mode is grounded in OLI with a fallback, the lighthouse plan has milestones with metrics and advance/pause/pivot signals with thresholds, the risk register has triggers and responsible parties, and the three critical decisions to make first are named.

USER:
I need a committee-ready international market entry strategy. The company wants to grow beyond its home market and must decide where to enter, in what order, and through what mode, without burning capital or closing the opportunity window.

Deliver: 1) screening of the candidate countries using CAGE relative to the home market plus size, growth, competitive intensity, and regulatory barriers, in an attractiveness-versus-ease-of-entry matrix with weights and traceable scores; 2) a wave sequence (lighthouse, expansion, opportunistic) following Uppsala, with the lighthouse market justified by lower risk and learning value; 3) entry mode per first-wave market using OLI and the control-versus-resources trade-off, with a recommended mode, fallback, and switching condition; 4) a lighthouse market plan with hypotheses to validate, product and value proposition adaptations, route to market, local partners or talent, minimum legal and tax requirements, 0-3-6-12 month milestones with metrics, and advance/pause/pivot signals; 5) comparative unit economics by mode in relative terms; and 6) a risk register (currency, regulatory, cultural, execution, reputational) with probability, impact, trigger, and responsible party. Close with the three critical decisions to make first.

Data:
